course: dictionary
lesson: 1137
title: Breeze: wind, ease and cheer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A breeze is usually a light, gentle wind, but the word can also mean something very easy. Look at the words around it to decide which meaning fits. «Leaves rock gently like a leaf in the breeze» is about air moving softly, so breeze means a light wind.
explain: Now read the next clue. «The test was a breeze» is not about air at all. A test cannot blow on your face, so breeze here means something very easy. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The test was a light wind" does not make sense, but "The test was very easy" does.
explain: Breeze can also be a verb. To breeze in or breeze through means to come in or get through something easily and cheerfully. «She breezed in, smiling» means she came in happily and without fuss. Look for the clue words that show how someone moves or feels.
fact: A breeze is a light, gentle wind.
fact: «A breeze» can also mean something very easy, as in «The test was a breeze».
fact: A sea breeze blows from the sea onto the land on sunny days; a land breeze blows out to sea at night.
fact: Sea breezes happen because the land warms faster than the sea on a sunny day, so warm air rises over the land and cooler sea air blows in.
fact: To breeze in or breeze through means to come in or get through something easily and cheerfully.
exercise: «The maths quiz was a breeze for Priya.» What does breeze mean here? | A small cloud | A cold gust of air | *Very easy | A loud noise
exercise: «She breezed in, smiling, and sat down.» What does breezed in mean? | Ran in angrily | *Came in easily and cheerfully | Fell over in the doorway | Blew in like a storm
exercise: Why does a sea breeze blow onto the land on a sunny day? | The breeze stops at night | Kites make the wind blow | The sea warms faster and pushes the air out | *The land warms faster than the sea, so cooler sea air blows in
test: «The ship sailed on, glad to feel the fresh breeze in its face.» What is the breeze? | A small boat | A heavy fog | *A light, gentle wind | A loud storm
test: «Ben breezed through his reading book in one afternoon.» What does breezed through mean? | *Got through it easily | Lost it in the park | Read it slowly, one word at a time | Tore it up angrily
exam: A sea breeze blows from the sea onto the land on sunny days. Where does a land breeze blow? | Nowhere, it never moves | Onto the land on sunny days | *Out to sea, usually at night | Up into the sky
